The [#4471 page](/kb/incidents/4471) calls every report one issue. The reports show two causes.
Each one got its own ticket. #4471 closed on 5 October.

<Callout>
The parser fix closed <code>parser</code> only. <code>hint</code> went away with the booking-v3 form.
</Callout>

## What was merged

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

| cause | ticket | outcome |
|---|---|---|
| `parser` | [tickets-mock #1](https://github.com/cprograms484/tickets-mock/issues/1) | fixed. `fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu locales` |
| `hint` | [tickets-mock #2](https://github.com/cprograms484/tickets-mock/issues/2) | won't fix. booking-v3 form retired |

- **parser.** The shared parser read every date month first. Day first tenants saw day and
  month flip. "7 April went out on 4 July."
- **hint.** The booking-v3 form printed `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field. People typed the
  month first, as told. The form read the day first. Only form reports from booking-v3 tenants.

The desk read a few `parser` reports as a one-day move, not a flip. They stay counted under
`parser`, footnoted under the table. [tickets-mock #3](https://github.com/cprograms484/tickets-mock/issues/3)
holds them in case one-day moves come back after the parser fix.

## How we counted

1. The export repeats some reports, because its pages overlap. Each report id counts once.
2. A report's cause comes from its tenant and the way it came in (`causes.ts`), not its intake
   tag. Intake guesses the tag from the browser language, so many tags point to the wrong cause.

## Why it was one ticket

Triage merges reports by subject line ([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)).
Both causes arrive with the same few subject lines, and triage never reads the body.
So they landed in one ticket.

